WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.199
This is a machining centre: a CNC milling machine that changes its own tools.

2
00:00:06.749 --> 00:00:11.488
At the side hangs the magazine, with dozens of cutters and drills.

3
00:00:12.038 --> 00:00:17.764
The program calls one up, and the changer puts it in the spindle in a few seconds.

4
00:00:18.664 --> 00:00:22.463
In milling the tool spins, and the part stands still.

5
00:00:23.013 --> 00:00:30.031
Here a block of aluminium, 100 by 80 by 25 millimetres, sits in a vice.

6
00:00:30.581 --> 00:00:33.994
The table moves left and right and back and forth,

7
00:00:34.194 --> 00:00:36.586
and the spindle moves up and down.

8
00:00:37.786 --> 00:00:43.849
The first tool, T1, is the face mill: a wide disc with inserts all around it.

9
00:00:44.749 --> 00:00:48.734
In two passes it makes the top flat, in 1 minute.

10
00:00:49.284 --> 00:00:53.208
That face is the starting point for every size still to come.

11
00:00:54.408 --> 00:00:59.122
Then T2, a end mill of 10 millimetres: a slim carbide rod,

12
00:00:59.322 --> 00:01:02.526
with cutting edges on its side and on its tip.

13
00:01:03.426 --> 00:01:06.805
It mills a pocket of 60 by 40 millimetres,

14
00:01:07.005 --> 00:01:12.151
8 millimetres deep: a recess with a floor, like a small tray in the part.

15
00:01:13.051 --> 00:01:16.778
T3 runs its side along the shape: that is the contour.

16
00:01:17.328 --> 00:01:20.498
A round cutter never makes a sharp inside corner.

17
00:01:21.047 --> 00:01:24.855
The corner gets as round as the cutter: here 4 millimetres.

18
00:01:26.055 --> 00:01:31.166
Now a steel block, with an end mill of 12 millimetres and 3 teeth,

19
00:01:31.366 --> 00:01:34.013
at 150 metres per minute.

20
00:01:34.563 --> 00:01:35.945
Just as in turning,

21
00:01:36.145 --> 00:01:42.600
the spindle speed follows from the diameter: 3,980 revolutions a minute.

22
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.205
Each tooth takes one bite per revolution.

23
00:01:46.755 --> 00:01:52.899
How thick it is, is the feed per tooth: here 0.05 millimetres.

24
00:01:53.799 --> 00:01:56.353
Times the teeth and the spindle speed,

25
00:01:56.553 --> 00:02:01.464
that gives the table feed: 597 millimetres per minute.

26
00:02:02.014 --> 00:02:04.382
That number is the F in the program.

27
00:02:05.282 --> 00:02:10.286
The cutter sits 3 millimetres sideways in the material: the width of cut.

28
00:02:11.186 --> 00:02:17.595
It removes 17.9 cubic centimetres of metal a minute: the metal removal rate.

29
00:02:18.145 --> 00:02:21.233
That number lets you compare two approaches fairly.

30
00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:23.826
Watch one tooth.

31
00:02:24.376 --> 00:02:28.393
In climb milling the tooth enters the material thick and leaves it thin.

32
00:02:29.293 --> 00:02:33.264
In conventional milling the tooth starts at zero thickness,

33
00:02:33.464 --> 00:02:35.902
and rubs over the surface first.

34
00:02:36.452 --> 00:02:38.205
That makes heat and wear.

35
00:02:38.755 --> 00:02:42.867
So on a modern CNC machine, climb milling is the standard.

36
00:02:44.067 --> 00:02:48.165
If a 10 millimetre cutter sits only 1 millimetre sideways,

37
00:02:48.365 --> 00:02:53.450
the chip gets thinner than the feed per tooth: only 0.6 times as thick.

38
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.382
That is chip thinning.

39
00:02:55.932 --> 00:02:58.370
The cutter then rubs instead of cutting.

40
00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:05.368
So you raise the feed per tooth, from 0.05 to 0.083.

41
00:03:05.918 --> 00:03:11.444
The feed then goes from 1,200 to 2,000 millimetres per minute,

42
00:03:11.644 --> 00:03:13.931
and the cutter actually lasts longer.

43
00:03:14.831 --> 00:03:18.059
That is how trochoidal milling works: small loops,

44
00:03:18.259 --> 00:03:22.508
only about 5 to 15 percent of the diameter sideways,

45
00:03:22.708 --> 00:03:25.239
along almost the whole cutting length.

46
00:03:25.789 --> 00:03:29.217
Heat and force stay low, at a much higher feed.

47
00:03:30.417 --> 00:03:34.109
A five-axis machine has two more rotary axes.

48
00:03:34.659 --> 00:03:39.445
Here the part sits on a trunnion table, which tilts and turns.

49
00:03:39.995 --> 00:03:43.513
That way the cutter reaches five sides in one setup.

50
00:03:44.413 --> 00:03:50.674
In 3+2 machining it tilts the part to a fixed angle, locks the rotary axes,

51
00:03:50.873 --> 00:03:54.325
and simply mills with X, Y and Z.

52
00:03:54.874 --> 00:03:56.836
That keeps the machine stiff.

53
00:03:57.736 --> 00:04:04.159
From 4 setups to 1: per batch, 3 times 30 is 90 minutes less changeover.

54
00:04:05.059 --> 00:04:09.647
In simultaneous 5-axis machining, all axes move at once.

55
00:04:10.198 --> 00:04:15.483
That keeps the cutter square to the domed face, as on the vanes of a pump impeller.
